  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8c575bff",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The three capital-letter features are strongly **right-skewed**: most emails have small values, and a few have extreme ones. Three signs show it:\n",
    "- **The mean is far above the median.** For `capital_run_length_longest`, half the emails have a longest run of 15 capitals or fewer, but the mean is about 52, pulled up by a few very large values.\n",
    "- **The maximum is huge compared with the middle.** One email has a run of 9,989 capitals, while 99% of emails are at or below 669. It is a single real email, not an error or a capped value, so we keep it.\n",
    "- **The skewness is very high** (about 10 to 32; 0 means symmetric, above 1 is already strongly skewed).\n",
    "\n",
    "On the histogram, almost all emails are squeezed into the first bar. After `log(1 + x)`, the values are spread much more evenly, and the skewness drops to about 0.1 to 3.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What we do with it:** in the pipeline, we apply `log(1 + x)` to these three features before scaling, so a few extreme emails don't dominate. We check in `analysis.ipynb` with cross-validation that this actually improves the model."
   ]
  },
