      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "                      % of ham  % of spam\n",
       "word_freq_george          26.3        0.4\n",
       "word_freq_hp              40.0        2.8\n",
       "word_freq_hpl             30.2        1.5\n",
       "word_freq_650             16.7        1.9\n",
       "word_freq_857              7.7        0.1\n",
       "word_freq_415              7.8        0.7\n",
       "word_freq_labs            17.5        1.0\n",
       "word_freq_telnet          11.2        0.1\n",
       "word_freq_technology      18.4        6.1"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 11,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "with open(CONFIG_PATH, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n",
    "    donor_cols = yaml.safe_load(f)[\"features\"][\"donor_columns\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "presence = (train_df[donor_cols] > 0).groupby(train_df[\"is_spam\"]).mean().T * 100\n",
    "presence.columns = [\"% of ham\", \"% of spam\"]\n",
    "presence.round(1)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "94a741b7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "These words appear in many legitimate emails and almost never in spam (for example, `hp` is in about 40% of ham and 3% of spam). They are very useful **on this dataset**, but only because they identify the donor's emails: they would mean nothing for GrandPied. Several are also strongly correlated with each other, as the next cell shows.\n",
    "\n",
    "We test in `analysis.ipynb` what happens if we drop them, and we report this dependence on one person's emails as a limitation of our model."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "5795df34",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "word_freq_857         word_freq_415           0.994\n",
       "                      word_freq_direct        0.837\n",
       "word_freq_415         word_freq_direct        0.833\n",
       "word_freq_857         word_freq_technology    0.699\n",
       "word_freq_415         word_freq_technology    0.695\n",
       "word_freq_telnet      word_freq_857           0.666\n",
       "word_freq_technology  word_freq_direct        0.664\n",
       "word_freq_labs        word_freq_technology    0.662\n",
       "word_freq_telnet      word_freq_415           0.662\n",
       "                      word_freq_technology    0.656\n",
       "word_freq_labs        word_freq_857           0.647\n",
       "                      word_freq_415           0.643\n",
       "word_freq_telnet      word_freq_direct        0.643\n",
       "word_freq_labs        word_freq_direct        0.608\n",
       "                      word_freq_telnet        0.601\n",
       "dtype: float64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 12,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# All pairs of features with a correlation above 0.6 (each pair listed once)\n",
    "corr = train_df.drop(columns=\"is_spam\").corr().abs()\n",
    "pairs = corr.where(np.triu(np.ones(corr.shape, dtype=bool), k=1)).stack()\n",
    "pairs[pairs > 0.6].sort_values(ascending=False).round(3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bf7501c1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Only one group of features is strongly correlated, and it is the donor's: `857` and `415` are almost identical (about 0.99), and both are also correlated with `direct`, `technology`, `telnet` and `labs`. No other pair goes above 0.6, so there is no wider collinearity problem. Regularisation in our models (L1/L2) handles this group; the L1 model indeed sets `857` and `415` to zero."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5e07ccd8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Target distribution"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "30fcc16d",
   "metadata": {
    "ExecuteTime": {
     "end_time": "2026-10-27T15:06:16.008825Z",
     "start_time": "2026-10-27T15:06:15.955863Z"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "y = train_df[\"is_spam\"]\n",
    "print(f\"Proportion of spam: {y.mean():.2%}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "y.value_counts(normalize=True).sort_index().plot(kind=\"bar\", color=[\"green\", \"red\"])\n",
    "plt.title(\"Distribution: Ham (0) vs Spam (1)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Proportion\")\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=0)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ],
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Proportion of spam: 39.88%\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
